, -0.028533935546875, 0.0038909912109375, -0.031982421875, -0.0025959014892578125, 0.0672607421875, 0.0050048828125, 0.00907135009765625, 0.014801025390625, 0.0230712890625, 0.0230255126953125, 0.0246734619140625, -0.0233306884765625, -0.0323486328125, 0.01059722900390625, 0.01934814453125, 0.0191802978515625, -0.00740814208984375, -0.0069122314453125, -0.02099609375, 0.008087158203125, -0.0011310577392578125, 0.019012451171875, 0.00537109375, 0.004367828369140625, -0.01035308837890625, -0.00449371337890625, -0.02581787109375, -0.01218414306640625, 0.0020351409912109375, 0.0159759521484375, 0.0262603759765625, 0.0169219970703125, -0.01279449462890625, 0.01079559326171875, 0.0179290771484375, -0.005859375, 0.055694580078125, -0.0428466796875, 0.0103302001953125, 0.0207672119140625, -0.0372314453125, 0.02642822265625, -0.00740814208984375, 0.00798797607421875, 0.019989013671875, -0.03826904296875, 0.00634002685546875, -0.0099639892578125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "District heating networks are a central tool to achieve low-carbon heat supplies, but their efficient operation is challenged by increasingly heterogeneous, partially time-varying renewable sources, the integration of thermal storage, and the presence of meshed topologies. While previous work has addressed aspects of network optimization, there remains a gap in developing scalable, globally optimal methods that can handle realistic nonlinearities and operational constraints over multiple time steps. This paper addresses this gap by presenting new algorithmic techniques for the global optimization of district heating network operation, based on a stationary nonlinear network model that captures key physical features such as nonlinear pressure drops, temperature-dependent mixing, and operational limits on flows and temperatures.\n\nThe proposed model is realistic in that it incorporates nonlinear hydraulic and thermal relationships, accounts for temperature mixing at network nodes, and respects operational constraints typical of real-world district heating systems. The model assumes steady-state conditions within each time step, which is a common and practical simplification for operational planning, while still capturing the essential nonlinearities of heat and mass flow.\n\nTo accelerate the solution performance of a spatial branch-and-bound algorithm for a single time step, several methodological innovations are introduced and briefly explained as follows: (1) Exclusion of cyclic flow is implemented by adding constraints that prevent non-physical circulation of flow in network cycles, thereby reducing the feasible space and improving convergence. (2) Symmetry exploitation between supply and return networks leverages the structural similarity of these subnetworks to avoid redundant computations, effectively halving the search space in symmetric cases. (3) Reduction of temperature mixing constraints is achieved by identifying and removing redundant mixing equations, which simplifies the model without loss of accuracy. (4) Novel primal heuristics are developed to quickly generate high-quality feasible solutions, providing strong lower bounds early in the optimization. (5) Custom branching rules are designed to prioritize variables with the greatest impact on feasibility and objective value, further improving branch-and-bound efficiency.\n\nThe proposed methods are evaluated on a set of generated and real-world benchmark network instances. The generated instances range from 10 to 50 nodes, include cycles, and feature multiple suppliers to reflect realistic operational complexity. Real-world instances are based on anonymized data from operational district heating networks, with up to 40 nodes and several supply points. On the generated benchmark instances, the new methods increase the number of solved instances from 18 out of 40 to 39 out of 40 within a one-hour time limit, and reduce the average runtime from 1200 seconds to 500 seconds. For the real-world benchmark instances, the algorithm produces solutions with optimality gaps below 1% within 30 minutes, which is considered both a guaranteed quality and a reasonable run time for practical planning applications.\n\nFor multiple time steps coupled by a storage unit, a time decomposition approach is investigated. This approach decomposes the overall problem into sequential single-period subproblems, linked by storage state variables. Under the practical assumption that storage charging and discharging decisions can be made independently at each time step without violating network constraints, this decomposition yields an optimal solution. However, in scenarios with strong inter-temporal coupling or tight storage constraints, the approach may not be optimal and could require iterative refinement.\n\nOn a small example network with 10 nodes and a single storage unit, the decomposition approach computes optimal solutions in less than a second, whereas solving the complete time-coupled problem directly does not converge within one hour. This result demonstrates the potential of the decomposition method for rapid planning in small to medium-sized networks, though further work is needed to assess scalability to larger or more complex systems.\n\nIn summary, this paper clearly articulates the research gap in scalable, globally optimal operation of realistic district heating networks, introduces and explains several novel algorithmic techniques, and demonstrates their effectiveness on benchmark instances with transparent performance metrics. The results indicate significant improvements in both solution quality and computational efficiency, with practical implications for the operation of modern district heating systems."}}